ANCHOR: /repo/app/lib/stores/workspaceStore.ts:528-543 (probe at :533-536, clobber at :539 → /repo/app/lib/stores/workspaceStore.ts:251-285 `migrateFromV2`); seam introduced at /repo/app/lib/stores/workspaceStore.ts:499 `storage: createJSONStorage(resolveWorkspaceStorage)`.

1. GUARD LOGIC (verbatim, :529-542) — the ONLY gate is the two localStorage probes; there is no "store is empty" check against the live/hydrated state, no flag check, and no async storage read:
```ts
return (_state, error) => {
  if (error) return;
  if (typeof window !== "undefined" && localStorage.getItem(WORKSPACE_KEY)) {
    const zustandRaw = localStorage.getItem("workspace-zustand-v1");
    let hasZustandData = false;
    try { hasZustandData = !!(zustandRaw && JSON.parse(zustandRaw)?.state?.sourceText); }
    catch { /* corrupted localStorage — proceed with migration */ }
    if (!hasZustandData) { migrateFromV2(); }
  }
};
```
`migrateFromV2` itself only guards on `const old = loadWorkspace(); if (!old) return false;` (:252-253) — i.e. a parseable `workspace-v2` blob with `version === 2` (/repo/app/lib/utils/workspacePersistence.ts:198-206). It then issues one unconditional `useWorkspaceStore.setState({...})` (:270-284) and never deletes `WORKSPACE_KEY`, so the condition never self-clears.

2. STORAGE SEAM IS REAL: /repo/app/lib/corpus/storeAdapter.ts `createCorpusBackedStorage` maps the persist key to `state/${name}.json` via CorpusFS (OPFS), and `resolveWorkspaceStorage()` picks it when `isCorpusEnabled()` (/repo/app/lib/corpus/flag.ts: `NEXT_PUBLIC_CORPUS_FS=1` or `localStorage["corpus-fs-enabled"]==="1"`). The branch's own test asserts the blob is NOT in localStorage under flag ON (/repo/app/lib/stores/__tests__/workspaceStore-corpus-flag.test.ts:81 `expect(localStorage.getItem("workspace-zustand-v1")).toBeNull()`), which is exactly the assumption line 534 depends on.

3. WRITE-BACK CONFIRMED: /repo/node_modules/zustand/esm/middleware.mjs:364-368 wraps `api.setState` as `savedSetState(...); return setItem();`, and `setItem()` (:357-363) calls `storage.setItem(options.name, ...)`. `migrateFromV2` uses `useWorkspaceStore.setState` (the wrapped api.setState), so the stale v2 snapshot is written back through the corpus adapter into OPFS — the clobber is persisted, not in-memory only.

4. TIMING CONFIRMED: middleware.mjs:411-431 — `postRehydrationCallback` is invoked in a `.then()` chained AFTER `set(stateFromStorage, true)` (:417-421), inside the `toThenable(storage.getItem...)` promise chain, so it runs after async OPFS hydration completes with `error === void 0`. The `if (error) return` branch only fires from the `.catch` at :432-437 (i.e. when hydration failed), so it does not suppress the success-path clobber.

5. FLAG-OFF PATH IS SAFE-ISH: with the flag off, `createDebouncedLocalStorage` writes the blob to `localStorage["workspace-zustand-v1"]`, so the probe reads the real blob and migration self-limits after the first successful migration (as long as `sourceText` is non-empty). Pre-existing residual quirk (NOT introduced here): if the migrated/persisted state has empty `sourceText`, `hasZustandData` stays false and migration re-runs on every load even with the flag off.

6. DIFF ATTRIBUTION: `git diff main...HEAD -- app/lib/stores/workspaceStore.ts` shows the ONLY changes are removal of the inline `createDebouncedStorage` and the swap to `storage: createJSONStorage(resolveWorkspaceStorage)`. `onRehydrateStorage` and `migrateFromV2` are byte-identical to main. So the diff introduced precisely the storage seam that invalidates the probe's assumption — the regression is attributable to this branch.

PRECISE PRECONDITIONS (all must hold):
- corpus flag ON (`NEXT_PUBLIC_CORPUS_FS=1` or `localStorage["corpus-fs-enabled"]==="1"`), AND
- a valid legacy `workspace-v2` localStorage entry with `version === 2` exists for the origin (written only by pre-zustand app versions; `saveWorkspace` at /repo/app/lib/utils/workspacePersistence.ts:83-109 has no remaining non-test callers, so this key is legacy residue that is never rewritten and never deleted), AND
- `localStorage["workspace-zustand-v1"]` is absent, unparseable, or has empty/missing `state.sourceText` — NOTE this is the main narrowing: a dev who previously ran the app flag-OFF with real content will still have that localStorage key populated, and `hasZustandData` will be true, suppressing the bug entirely.

CORRECTED ONE-SENTENCE FAILURE SCENARIO: With the corpus flag on, a stale `workspace-v2` key present, and no usable `workspace-zustand-v1` entry left in localStorage, every page load hydrates state from OPFS (`state/workspace-zustand-v1.json`) and then the post-rehydration callback — blind to the OPFS seam — runs `migrateFromV2()`, whose `setState` both overwrites the freshly hydrated state and, via zustand's wrapped `api.setState → setItem`, rewrites the stale legacy snapshot back into OPFS, and since the `workspace-v2` key is never removed this repeats on every reload.

ANCILLARY: no test in the diff exercises `onRehydrateStorage` under flag ON; /repo/app/lib/stores/__tests__/workspaceStore-corpus-flag.test.ts only tests the adapter routing, and /repo/app/lib/stores/__tests__/workspaceStore-characterization.test.ts covers the OFF path — so the seam/probe interaction is untested.

